    "q-37056": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37056",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/d65013cac950b3e3d533dd6dfbdf1dc6582c10fcb7d80f00ab559935915d9595.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local maximum?\n\nI. $x=-1$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local maximum at $x=-1.$ For example, in the interval $(-1.25,-0.75),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=-1,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/a5f0e23d8bed98f693ee72086b0bf17b9f65de8931bba6f16cfcc34e670a757e.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(-1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-1.25,-0.75).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local maximum at $x=1.$ For any open interval containing $x=1,$ there will be points on the graph that lie above $f(1).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local maximum at $x=3.$ For any open interval containing $x=3,$ there will be points on the graph that lie above $f(3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-37056-a",
