    "q-108276": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108276",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/d71ebdb084e23aef4b3901aaba2e4bb4de5438fa41a3fc331bef749f3c78de76.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=-4$\n\nII. $x=-1$\n\nIII. $x=3$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-4.$ For any open interval containing $x=-4$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-4).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-1.$ For any open interval containing $x=-1$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-1).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/bfe274fe02b7200164eaad5c98aa2b19297738987f70059157d2895b989a962c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
