    "q-108273": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108273",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/69217ab9fc86fdbb424a0716f00596197b7da9e930c74b12b82406ce2ee8354c.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=-2$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=4$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For any open interval containing $x=-2,$ there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/8e56e79caf5c7903de977ee66894961b6068e3974ffd9ff4482b2086fad290ef.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=4.$ For any open interval containing $x=4,$ there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
