    "q-90634": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90634",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/d13474a62324af5edcaafd10e60c6b02c257f32c015d5769512725d157e01420.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=5$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(7)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/32c866706140c0bc9a40bcb0ffdca4a827578e2d16dbcefc2cdb66b5c05b1c1c.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=5.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(5).$ For example, $f(0)<f(5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
